@startuml write-path
title US-01: Write Path - Position Ping Ingested

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

collections "OpenSky / AIS\nFeed" as Feed
participant "Ingestion\nPoller" as Poller
queue "Kafka\nadsb.raw / ais.raw" as KafkaRaw
participant "Position\nConsumer" as Consumer
queue "Kafka\nposition.normalized" as KafkaNorm
database "Redis\nentity:live:{id}" as Redis
database "TimescaleDB\nposition_history" as TSDB

Feed -> Poller : position ping\n(entity_id, lat, lon, timestamp_ms)
Poller -> KafkaRaw : publish to adsb.raw / ais.raw

note over KafkaRaw
  Decouples poller from consumers.
  Absorbs feed bursts without back-pressure.
  Malformed events are routed to DLQ - not shown here (see US-09).
end note

KafkaRaw -> Consumer : consume raw event (at-least-once)
Consumer -> Consumer : parse and normalise\n(validate fields, convert units)
Consumer -> KafkaNorm : publish to position.normalized

Consumer -> Redis : HSET entity:live:{entity_id}\n{ lat, lon, altitude, last_seen_ms }

note right of Redis
  TTL = 24h (safety-net only).
  Must be longer than SIGNAL_LOSS_THRESHOLD_MS
  so the alert evaluator can always find the key
  on its scan cycle. Dashboard ghost cleanup is
  client-side: Angular removes the marker when
  now() - last_seen_ms > SIGNAL_LOSS_THRESHOLD_MS.
end note

Consumer -> Redis : PUBLISH position-updates\n{ entity_id, lat, lon, altitude, last_seen_ms }

note right of Redis
  All API instances subscribe to position-updates.
  Each fans out to scoped WebSocket connections.
  Enables horizontal API scaling without
  additional pub/sub infrastructure.
end note

Consumer -> TSDB : INSERT INTO position_history\nON CONFLICT (entity_id, observed_at) DO NOTHING

note right of TSDB
  Conflict target: (entity_id, observed_at).
  TimescaleDB requires the partition column in
  any unique constraint on a hypertable.
  observed_at = to_timestamp(timestamp_ms / 1000.0).
  Safe to replay from Kafka offset.
end note

@enduml
